### Step 1: Install Nginx
First, ensure that Nginx is installed. If it's not installed, you can do so with the following command:

```bash
sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y nginx
```

### Step 2: Generate a Self-Signed SSL Certificate
Instead of using a certificate that might not exist, we'll generate a self-signed SSL certificate and key:

```bash
sudo openssl req -x509 -nodes -days 365 -newkey rsa:2048 -keyout /etc/nginx/ssl/mywebsite.key -out /etc/nginx/ssl/mywebsite.crt
```

During the certificate generation, you will be prompted to enter information such as country, state, city, organization, etc. You can use default values if you're just testing.

### Step 3: Create a New Nginx Configuration File
Create a new configuration file for your site. For example, let's create a configuration file for a site called `mywebsite`.

```bash
sudo nano /etc/nginx/sites-available/mywebsite
```

Add the following configuration to the file:

```nginx
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name yourdomain.com;
    return 301 https://$host$request_uri;
}

server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name yourdomain.com;

    ssl_certificate /etc/nginx/ssl/mywebsite.crt;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/nginx/ssl/mywebsite.key;

    ssl_protocols TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;
    ssl_ciphers 'ECDHE-ECDSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384:ECDHE-RSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384:ECDHE-ECDSA-CHACHA20-POLY1305:ECDHE-RSA-CHACHA20-POLY1305:ECDHE-ECDSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256:ECDHE-RSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256';
    ssl_prefer_server_ciphers on;

    root /var/www/html;
    index index.html index.htm;

    location / {
        try_files $uri $uri/ =404;
    }
}
```

Replace `yourdomain.com` with your actual domain name.

### Step 4: Enable the New Configuration
Create a symbolic link to enable the new configuration:

```bash
sudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/mywebsite /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/
```

### Step 5: Test Nginx Configuration
Test the Nginx configuration to ensure there are no syntax errors:

```bash
sudo nginx -t
```

If the test passes, you should see output indicating that the configuration is successful.

### Step 6: Restart Nginx
Restart Nginx to apply the changes:

```bash
sudo systemctl restart nginx
```

### Step 7: Access the Site
You should now be able to access your site over HTTPS using the self-signed certificate. If you encounter any issues, check the Nginx error logs for more information:

```bash
sudo tail -f /var/log/nginx/error.log
```

This should help you diagnose and fix any problems related to the Nginx configuration and SSL certificates.
